---
/**
 * CLI reference. Authored here, and read from the built binary rather than
 * written from memory: every block below is the binary's own output at build
 * time. The exit-code table is asserted against real runs, so the build fails
 * if the CLI's behaviour and this page part ways.
 */
import Terminal from '../components/Terminal.astro';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { lore, session } from '../lib/lore';

const help = lore('--help');
const version = lore('--version');
const usage = {
  init: lore('init'),
  capture: lore('capture'),
  search: lore('search'),
};
const [refusal] = session([['capture', 'brain', 'a thought']]);
const unknown = lore('frobnicate');

const EXIT = [
  {
    code: 0,
    meaning:
      'It did what was asked. This includes a capture that found the URL already filed, and a search that matched nothing.',
    run: version,
  },
  {
    code: 1,
    meaning:
      'It refused, and wrote nothing. The message says why and what to do.',
    run: refusal,
  },
  {
    code: 2,
    meaning:
      'The command was incomplete or malformed. The message repeats the usage line.',
    run: usage.search,
  },
];
for (const row of EXIT) {
  if (row.run?.code !== row.code) {
    throw new Error(
      `cli: expected exit ${row.code} from \`${row.run?.command}\`, got ${row.run?.code}.`,
    );
  }
}
if (unknown.code !== 1)
  throw new Error(`cli: an unknown argument now exits ${unknown.code}.`);
---
<Base href="/cli/" heading="CLI reference">
  <p class="lede">Three commands. This is the whole surface, as the built binary prints it.</p>

  <h2 id="help"><code>lore --help</code></h2>
  <Terminal runs={[help]} />

  <h2 id="version"><code>lore --version</code></h2>
  <Terminal runs={[version]} />
  <p>Prints the version and nothing else, so scripts can read it.</p>

  <h2 id="init"><code>lore init &lt;dir&gt;</code></h2>
  <p>Creates a brain at <code>dir</code>, or adopts the vault already there. It is safe to run again. See <a href="/knowledge-structure/">Knowledge structure</a> and <a href="/adoption/">Existing vaults</a>.</p>
  <Terminal runs={[usage.init]} showCode />

  <h2 id="capture"><code>lore capture &lt;brain&gt; &lt;item&gt;</code></h2>
  <p>Files one thought into <code>inbox/</code>, or one http or https URL into <code>sources/</code>. Quote the item. See <a href="/capture/">Capture</a>.</p>
  <Terminal runs={[usage.capture]} showCode />

  <h2 id="search"><code>lore search &lt;brain&gt; &lt;query&gt;…</code></h2>
  <p>Ranks the spans of <code>brain</code> against one or more wordings, each in its own quotes. It reads any directory of Markdown and writes nothing. See <a href="/retrieval/">Retrieval</a>.</p>
  <Terminal runs={[usage.search]} showCode />
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Flag</th><th scope="col">Effect</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>--json</code></th><td>The same results as JSON: <code>path</code>, <code>anchor</code>, <code>startLine</code>, <code>endLine</code>, <code>score</code>, <code>text</code> per result.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>--limit &lt;n&gt;</code></th><td>How many results come back. A whole number, at least 1.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="exit-codes">Exit codes</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Code</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th><th scope="col">For example</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {EXIT.map((row) => (
          <tr><th scope="row"><code>{row.code}</code></th><td>{row.meaning}</td><td><code>{row.run?.command}</code></td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>An argument <code>lore</code> does not know exits 1:</p>
  <Terminal runs={[unknown]} showCode />

  <h2 id="environment">Colour and output</h2>
  <p>At a terminal that can show colour, <code>lore --help</code> sets the name in the accent and closes with the serial <code>LK-047</code>. Nothing else is coloured, and nothing a script reads is touched: <code>--version</code>, <code>--json</code>, every error, and every run that is piped or has <code>NO_COLOR</code> or <code>TERM=dumb</code> set keeps the same bytes. Results go to standard output; refusals go to standard error.</p>
  <p><code>lore</code> runs offline, takes no credentials, and makes no network call.</p>
</Base>
